content.thinking:
The dispose-before-register test now times out waiting for `plugin.startCalls > 0` — because the seed-path guard `if (_dispose != null) return;` blocks `_connectWithScopedBoot` BEFORE `_startHub()`/`_tryScopedBootConnect`, so the plugin never starts. That's the intended behavior of the guard (a disposed host doesn't boot the client), but my test asserted startCalls > 0 as a proxy for "boot completed" — wrong proxy.

Better: wait for something else — e.g., the overlay NOT existing after a settle delay, and drop the startCalls wait. Simply: after register, delay ~100ms, assert overlay doesn't exist. But without waiting for boot to have started, the assertion is weak (the seed might not have run yet even without the guard). Hmm — with the guard, `_connect` runs but returns early; without the guard (pre-fix), boot seeds the overlay quickly.

Robust approach: wait until either the overlay exists OR a boot-connect marker... In the pre-fix code, what observable signals boot? `plugin.startCalls > 0` via `_startHub()` (status not connected → tries start). With the guard, none of that happens — which IS the point.

For a deterministic pre/post-fix discriminator: wait a bounded time for the overlay to appear; pre-fix it appears (test then fails on the expect isFalse), post-fix it never appears. Use _waitUntil on overlay.exists with INVERTED logic:

```dart
// Pre-fix the boot seeds the overlay a few ms after register; give it
// a bounded window to appear, then assert it never did.
final overlayAppeared = await _waitUntilOrFalse(() async => overlay.exists());
expect(overlayAppeared, isFalse);
```

I'll add a helper `_waitUntilOrFalse` that returns bool instead of throwing. Actually simpler: reuse _waitUntil with try/catch:

```dart
var overlayAppeared = true;
try {
 await _waitUntil(() async => overlay.exists());
} on StateError {
 overlayAppeared = false;
}
expect(overlayAppeared, isFalse);
```

Pre-fix: overlay appears → _waitUntil returns → overlayAppeared stays true → expect fails (RED confirmed earlier — it failed at the exists expect, good). Post-fix: _waitUntil throws after 2s → false → pass. The 2s delay is a bit slow but acceptable (one test).

Actually — even better signal that boot RAN in the post-fix world: `fixture`-style `_waitUntil(() async => plugin.startCalls > 0)` won't fire... what does register do synchronously? It registers the session-ready listener and calls `unawaited(_connect(...))`. With the guard, _connect returns immediately. So no signal. Use the try/catch approach.

